d in 2000 and trends up to 2001. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 27:193&#x02013;200 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14718903</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Freedman</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name><name><surname>Vale</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalka</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Kearney</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pieczek</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Symes</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Losordo</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Isner</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Plasma vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) levels after intramuscular and intramyocardial gene transfer of VEGF-1 plasmid DNA</article-title><source>Hum Gene Ther</source><year>2002</year><volume>13</volume><fpage>1595</fpage><lpage>1603</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1089/10430340260201680</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Freedman SB, Vale P, Kalka C, Kearney M, Pieczek A, Symes J, Losordo D, Isner JM (2002) Plasma vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) levels after intramuscular and intramyocardial gene transfer of VEGF-1 plasmid DNA. Hum Gene Ther 13:1595&#x02013;1603 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12228014</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gerritsen</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>HGF and VEGF: a dynamic duo</article-title><source>Circ Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>96</volume><fpage>272</fpage><lpage>273</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1161/01.RES.0000157575.66295.e0</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Gerritsen ME (2005) HGF and VEGF: a dynamic duo. Circ Res 96:272&#x020